.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt KACHE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kache
.Nd multi threaded, file backed cache with an HTTP interface
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl mPHnMAFqvh
.Op Fl l Ar addr
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl s Ar size
.Op Fl S Ar shards
.Op Fl t Ar threads
.Op Fl c Ar conns
.Op Fl e Ar ttl
.Op Fl y Ar ms
.Op Fl i Ar idle
.Op Fl b Ar backlog
.Op Fl B Ar bytes
.Op Fl K Ar bytes
.Op Fl V Ar bytes
.Op Fl X Ar ms
.Op Fl C Ar nodes
.Op Fl N Ar index
.Op Fl Y Ar ms
.Op Fl U Ar addrs
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
keeps a cache in a file mapped
.Dv MAP_SHARED ,
so the data lives in the page cache while it is hot and survives a
restart without a serialisation step.  Entries carry an optional time to
live and a version used for compare and swap.  Clients speak HTTP/1.1.
.Pp
A key holds one of three things: a value, a nested map of fields, or a
queue.  A container carries its own time to live and so does every item
in it, independently.  Because a container lives entirely inside the
shard its key hashes to, a multi field write, a whole map enumeration
and a move between two queues are each atomic under one lock.
.Pp
The store is divided into independently locked shards, each with its own
hash index and allocation arena.  When a shard runs out of room it evicts
approximately least recently used entries, preferring ones that have
already expired.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl l Ar addr
Address to listen on.  Default
.Ql 0.0.0.0 .
.It Fl p Ar port
Port to listen on.  Default
.Ql 7070 .
.It Fl f Ar file
Backing file.  Created if absent.  Default
.Ql kache.db .
.It Fl s Ar size
Size of the backing file, with an optional
.Ql k , m , g
or
.Ql t
suffix.  This is roughly how much the cache holds.  Default
.Ql 1G .
.It Fl S Ar shards
Number of shards, rounded down to a power of two.  Zero derives one from
the file size.  Default zero.
.It Fl t Ar threads
Worker threads.  Zero means one per CPU.  Default zero.
.It Fl c Ar conns
Maximum concurrent connections per thread.  Default
.Ql 8192 .
.It Fl e Ar ttl
Time to live in seconds applied to requests that do not carry one.  Zero
means entries do not expire by default.  Default zero.
.It Fl y Ar ms
How often to
.Xr msync 2
the mapping in the background.  Zero leaves writeback entirely to the
kernel.  Default
.Ql 1000 .
.It Fl i Ar idle
Seconds a connection may sit idle before it is closed.  The activity
stamp on a connection is only refreshed every
.Dv CFG_TOUCH_MS ,
so a connection may be closed up to that much early, never late.
Default
.Ql 60 .
.It Fl b Ar backlog
Listen backlog.  Default
.Ql 1024 .
.It Fl B Ar bytes
Expected size of a stored item, header and key included.  Enough buckets
are provisioned that an arena full of items this size can still be
indexed at the table's load factor, so it decides the split between index
and data and nothing else.  Default
.Ql 128 .
.It Fl K Ar bytes
Largest key accepted.  Default
.Ql 255 .
.It Fl V Ar bytes
Largest value accepted.  Also bounds how many shards fit, since a shard's
arena must hold several maximum sized items.  Default
.Ql 256k .
.It Fl m
Lock the mapping into memory with
.Xr mlock 2
so it can never be paged out.  Needs a sufficient
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK .
.It Fl P
Fault the whole file in at startup instead of on demand.
.It Fl H
Advise the kernel that the mapping may use transparent huge pages.
.It Fl n
Discard any existing contents and create the store afresh.  Required to
change the geometry of an existing file.
.It Fl M
Minimal responses.  The
.Ql Server
header is dropped from every response,
.Ql Connection: keep-alive
is dropped when the connection is in fact being kept alive, and a
successful
.Cm GET
of
.Ar /kv/<key>
also drops
.Ql Content-Type
and the
.Ql ETag ,
.Ql X-Kache-TTL
and
.Ql X-Kache-Flags
trio.  A
.Cm HEAD
of the same key keeps them: it returns no body, so the elision would
save nothing, and metadata is the whole reason to ask.
.Ql Connection: close
is still always sent.  Responses to writes keep every header they
otherwise would, so compare and swap and conditional requests still
work; an HTTP/1.0 client, which needs the keep alive echo, is never
answered minimally either.
.It Fl X Ar ms
Answer hot keys from a per worker set of finished responses, bypassing
the store entirely.  A key is admitted only after it has been asked for
often enough inside one decay window, so a keyspace with no hot key in
it admits nothing.
.Ar ms
is how long an entry stands, and therefore how stale a read may be; a
write retires the set of the worker that took it, so a client still
reads its own writes.
.Ql 0
disables it, which is the default.
.It Fl C Ar nodes
Run as one node of a cluster, given every member as
.Ql host:port
separated by commas, in the same order on every node.  Each node keeps a
full copy, so a read is answered locally by whichever node it reaches.
A key's writes belong to one owner, computed from the hash, and a write
arriving at any other node is answered
.Ql 307
with the owner in
.Ql Location .
The hash seed is derived from this list, so a store created under a
different one is refused rather than serving a keyspace its peers
disagree about.  Maps and queues are not replicated.
.It Fl N Ar index
This node's position in the
.Fl C
list, counting from zero.  The default is 0.
.It Fl Y Ar ms
How often a node ships what it has written to its peers, default 50.
Writes to one key inside an interval are coalesced into a single frame
carrying the last value, so peer traffic follows this interval and not
the write rate.  It is also the staleness bound.
.It Fl U Ar addrs
The address of each
.Fl C
node as a client should reach it, in the same order, when that differs
from the address the nodes use between themselves - behind NAT, inside
containers, or under a proxy.  Only redirects are affected: replication
keeps using the
.Fl C
addresses.  Without it a redirect names the peer address, which a client
on the other side of the boundary cannot resolve.
.It Fl A
Pin each worker to one cpu,
.Li i % ncpu ,
so its stack and its connection buffers stay on the core that faulted
them in.  Worth having when nothing else on the machine wants those
cpus, and worth not having when something does.  A kernel that refuses
is warned about, not fatal.
.It Fl F
Enable
.Ql POST /flush .
Without it that endpoint answers 403.
.It Fl q
Quiet; suppress informational messages.
.It Fl v
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.El
.Sh HTTP INTERFACE
.Bl -tag -width "POST /prepend/<key>" -compact
.It Cm GET Ar /kv/<key>
Fetch.  The
.Ql ETag
is the compare and swap token.
.It Cm HEAD Ar /kv/<key>
Metadata only.
.It Cm PUT Ar /kv/<key>
Store the request body.
.It Cm POST Ar /kv/<key>
As
.Cm PUT .
.It Cm DELETE Ar /kv/<key>
Remove.
.It Cm POST Ar /incr/<key>
Add
.Ql ?by=N
to a numeric value.
.It Cm POST Ar /decr/<key>
Subtract
.Ql ?by=N .
.It Cm POST Ar /append/<key>
Append the body.
.It Cm POST Ar /prepend/<key>
Prepend the body.
.It Cm POST Ar /touch/<key>
Reset the time to live.
.It Cm POST Ar /mget
One percent encoded key per line; the reply frames each value as its
length, a newline and that many bytes, or
.Ql -1
for a miss.
.It Cm POST Ar /mset
Records of
.Ql <key> <bytes> [ttl]
followed by that many bytes.
.It Cm POST Ar /mdel
One key per line.
.It Cm POST Ar /flush
Drop everything.
.It Cm GET Ar /stats
Counters, one per line.
.It Cm GET Ar /metrics
The same in Prometheus form.
.It Cm GET Ar /health
Liveness.
.El
.Pp
Nested maps.
.Ql ?f=<field>
names a field, percent encoded exactly like a key.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm GET Ar /kkv/<key>
Every live field, framed as
.Ql <fieldlen> <valuelen> <ttl>
followed by the field bytes and then the value bytes.  At most
.Ql ?n=
of them;
.Ql ?n=0
returns the counters alone.
.It Cm GET Ar /kkv/<key>?f=<field>
One field.  The
.Ql ETag
and
.Ql X-Kache-TTL
describe the field, not the map.
.It Cm PUT Ar /kkv/<key>?f=<field>
Store one field, creating the map if needed.
.It Cm POST Ar /kkv/<key>
Many fields under one hold of the lock, in the same frames.
.It Cm DELETE Ar /kkv/<key>?f=<field>
Remove one field; without
.Ql ?f=
the whole map.
.It Cm POST Ar /kkvdel/<key>
Many fields, framed as
.Ql <len>
and the name bytes.
.It Cm POST Ar /kkvincr/<key>?f=<field>
Add
.Ql ?by=
to a field, as
.Ar /incr
does to a key.
.Ar /kkvdecr
subtracts.
.It Cm POST Ar /kkvtouch/<key>
A new ttl for
.Ql ?f=
or, without it, for the map.
.El
.Pp
Queues.
.Ql ?side=l
or
.Ql ?side=r
names an end; pushes default to the right and pops to the left, which
makes the plain calls a FIFO.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm POST Ar /q/<key>
Push the body as one message.
.It Cm POST Ar /qpush/<key>
Push many, framed as
.Ql <valuelen> [ttl]
and the bytes.
.It Cm POST Ar /qpop/<key>
Pop one, answering with its bytes, or
.Ql ?n=
of them, framed.  204 when there is nothing to take.
.It Cm GET Ar /q/<key>
The same without removing anything.
.It Cm POST Ar /qmove/<key>?dst=<key>
Take one message off this queue and put it on another, under both locks
at once, and answer with it.  The message is never in neither queue.
.It Cm POST Ar /qtrim/<key>?maxlen=<n>
Keep at most that many.
.It Cm POST Ar /qtouch/<key>
A new ttl for the queue.
.It Cm DELETE Ar /q/<key>
Remove the queue.
.El
.Pp
.Ql ?ttl=<seconds>
or
.Ql ?ttlms=<milliseconds>
sets the expiry, zero meaning never.
.Ql If-None-Match: *
stores only when the key is absent and
.Ql If-Match: \(dq<etag>\(dq
only when the value has not changed since it was read; both answer 412
when the condition fails.  Every operation is atomic with respect to
every other operation on the same key.
.Pp
The batch endpoints exist because a round trip costs far more than a
lookup does.
.Ar /mget , /mset
and
.Ar /mdel
are not atomic across keys: each key is taken under its own shard lock in
turn.  The container batches are, because every item in one container is
under one lock.
.Pp
.Ql ?kttl=<seconds>
sets a container's own expiry, which is independent of the items in it
and is applied only when the request carries it, so writing one field
does not restart the clock on the map.  A container that loses its last
item stops existing.
.Pp
Deleting a container is constant time whatever its size: it leaves the
index at once and is taken apart afterwards by the requests that follow
it and by a once a second housekeeping tick.
.Ql kache_reclaim_pending
counts what is still waiting.  An allocation that cannot be met reclaims
all of it before evicting anything a client can still read.
.Sh SECURITY
.Nm
has no authentication and no transport security: anything that can open a
connection can read and write every key.
.Fl F
gates
.Ql POST /flush
because it cannot be undone, but every other write is already unguarded.
.Pp
On a node started with
.Fl C
the endpoint
.Ql POST /x/repl
applies a peer's writes directly, without the ownership check that
answers a client with a 307.  Nothing identifies the sender, so a request
to it from anywhere else is indistinguishable from one from a peer, and
anything that can reach the port can therefore write any key on every
node of the cluster.  Bind the port where only the peers and your own
clients can reach it
.Pq Fl l ,
or firewall it.  Where clients cannot reach the nodes on the address the
peers use, see
.Fl U
and
.Xr kache-lb 1 .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa config.def.h
Compile time defaults and limits.  Copied to
.Pa config.h
by the build, which is the file to edit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Serve a four gigabyte cache with a default expiry of five minutes:
.Pp
.Dl $ kache -f /var/tmp/kache.db -s 4G -e 300
.Pp
Store and read a value:
.Pp
.Dl $ curl -X PUT -d hello localhost:7070/kv/greeting
.Dl $ curl localhost:7070/kv/greeting
.Pp
A map with a day on the key and a minute on one of its fields:
.Pp
.Dl $ curl -X PUT -d alice localhost:7070/kkv/user:1?f=name&kttl=86400
.Dl $ curl -X PUT -d tok localhost:7070/kkv/user:1?f=session&ttl=60
.Pp
A worker taking one job off a queue without being able to lose it:
.Pp
.Dl $ curl -X POST 'localhost:7070/qmove/work?dst=work:inflight'
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
On startup after an unclean shutdown
.Nm
reports that it is rebuilding the index, and how many records survived.
A shard whose block chain fails validation is reset and reported
separately.
.Sh BUGS
Only one process may have a store file open at a time, which is enforced
with an advisory lock.  Durability is best effort: writes reach disk when
the kernel or
.Fl y
says so, and a crash can lose the most recent ones.
